\par\pard\plain \ltrch\loch {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "http://www.addthis.com/bookmark.php?v=300&pubid=jtimes"}{\fldrslt\f2\fs24\b0\ul\ulc0\cf1 SHARE }}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb240\ltrch\loch {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 H}{\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 IROSHIMA \endash  }{\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The atomic blast in Hiroshima on Aug. 6, 1945, killed 140,000 people; tens of thousands died instantly, while the rest succumbed to injuries or illness in the weeks, months and years afterwards.}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb280\ltrch\loch {\f1\fs28\b1\cf1 Ball of fire}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb240\ltrch\loch {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The first thing people noticed was an \loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2\uc1\u8220\'93intense ball of fire\u8221\'94 according to the International Committee of the Red Cross (ICRC).}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The atomic bomb had a yield of 15 kilotons, equal to 15,000 tons of TNT, yet was 3,300 times less powerful than the biggest hydrogen bomb tested by the Soviet Union in 1961.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Temperatures at the epicenter of the blast reached an estimated 7,000 degrees Celsius (12,600 Fahrenheit), which caused fatal burns within a radius of about 5 kilometers (3 miles).}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 ICRC experts say there were cases of temporary or permanent blindness due to the intense flash of light, and subsequent related damage such as cataracts.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 A whirlwind of heat generated by the explosion also ignited thousands of fires that burned several square kilometers of the largely wooden city. A firestorm that consumed all available oxygen caused more deaths by suffocation.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 It has been estimated that burn- and fire-related casualties accounted for more than half of the immediate deaths in Hiroshima.}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb280\ltrch\loch {\f1\fs28\b1\cf1 Shock wave}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb240\ltrch\loch {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The explosion generated an enormous shock wave and almost instantaneous expansion of air, which also caused a huge number of deaths.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Some people were literally blown away, while others were crushed inside collapsed buildings or perforated by flying debris.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The ICRC recorded many victims with ruptured internal organs, open fractures, broken skulls and penetration wounds.}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb280\ltrch\loch {\f1\fs28\b1\cf1 Radiation}
\par\pard\plain \sa240\sb240\ltrch\loch {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Another deadly effect of the atomic bomb was the emission of radiation that proved harmful in both the short and long term.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Radiation sickness was reported in the attack\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2\uc1\u8217\'92s aftermath by many who survived the initial blast and firestorm.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Acute radiation symptoms include vomiting, headaches, nausea, diarrhoea, hemorrhaging and hair loss. Radiation sickness can lead to death within a few weeks or months.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Longer-term effects noted among hibakusha are increased risks of thyroid cancer or leukemia.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 In both Hiroshima and Nagasaki, which was hit by an atomic bomb on Aug. 9, 1945, the rates of various cancers and leukemia have risen.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 Of 50,000 radiation victims from both cities studied by the Japanese-U.S. Radiation Effects Research Foundation, about 100 died of leukemia and 850 suffered from radiation-induced cancers.}
\par\plain {\f2\fs24\b0\cf1 The foundation found no evidence of a \loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2\uc1\u8220\'93significant increase\u8221\'94 in serious birth defects among survivors\u8217\'92 children, however.}}